ntal Technologies (UIC ERC)","url":"https://d35t1syewk4d42.cloudfront.net/file/1462/2012-Corn-Ethanol-Emerging-Plant-Energy-and-Environmental-Technologies.pdf","note":"Sum of the listed motor loads. Reads low: corn oil extraction, chillers and the thermal oxidiser are not modelled, and real plants run motors well below their best efficiency point.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":20,"pass":false},{"metric":"DDG yield, dry basis","model":14.617144,"published":15.73,"unit":"lb/bu","error_pct":-7.074733,"inside_range":null,"source":"Mueller and Kwik, 2012 Corn Ethanol: Emerging Plant Energy and Environmental Technologies (UIC ERC)","url":"https://d35t1syewk4d42.cloudfront.net/file/1462/2012-Corn-Ethanol-Emerging-Plant-Energy-and-Environmental-Technologies.pdf","note":"Model DDG excludes yeast cell mass, which is why it reads a little low.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":10,"pass":true},{"metric":"Fresh water use","model":2.754689,"published":2.7,"unit":"gal/gal","error_pct":2.025516,"inside_range":null,"source":"Mueller and Kwik, 2012 Corn Ethanol: Emerging Plant Energy and Environmental Technologies (UIC ERC)","url":"https://d35t1syewk4d42.cloudfront.net/file/1462/2012-Corn-Ethanol-Emerging-Plant-Energy-and-Environmental-Technologies.pdf","note":"Process make up plus cooling tower evaporation and blowdown; the plant recycles all condensate.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":30,"pass":true},{"metric":"Plant construction cost per annual gallon (2024 USD)","model":1.3580088246425766,"published":3.21,"unit":"USD/gal","error_pct":-57.731170825402266,"inside_range":null,"source":"farmdoc daily (University of Illinois), 4 March 2026, representative Iowa ethanol plant model","url":"https://farmdocdaily.illinois.edu/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/fdd030426.pdf","note":"Published figure is $2.11 per gallon for a 100 million gallon dry mill built in 2007, escalated here to the 2024 cost basis with CEPCI (525.4 to 800). Shown for information: dry mills were built under fixed price design build contracts, so factored estimates scatter widely around this figure.","kind":"cost","tolerance_pct":null,"pass":null}],"assumptions":["Corn: 56 lb per bushel at 15.5% moisture; dry matter 72.0% starch and 2.6% simple sugars (Purdue ID-328, Table 1). The rest (protein, fibre, oil, ash) passes through to DDGS.","98% of the starch is hydrolysed to glucose and 95% of the glucose is fermented at the theoretical 0.511 g ethanol per g glucose; the rest of the glucose stands for yeast growth and glycerol and ends up in DDGS.","Corn oil extraction, which most plants now run, is not modelled; the oil stays in the DDGS.","Fermentation CO2 is biogenic and is vented after a water scrubber; it is shown as a by-product and is not counted as fossil CO2. Many plants sell it to CO2 merchants.","Steam from an on site gas boiler (85% efficient on LHV); DDGS dryers are gas fired at 3,300 kJ per kg water removed.","Beer column overhead 50 wt% ethanol; the rectifier runs on beer column vapour and its condensers feed the 4 effect thin stillage evaporators.","Wet cake 35% solids, syrup 35% solids, 30% of thin stillage recycled as backset, DDGS at 10% moisture.","Denaturant blending, enzyme and yeast costs and chemicals are not itemised.","Fermenters are field erected tanks costed on the fixed roof tank correlation; grain handling is an allowance."],"notes":[]}